  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances are expected\nthrough early next week.\n\nConvection should wane after midnight with loss of heating and\nforcing.\n\nA deep southerly flow will continue around high pressure over \nthe Atlantic, ushering plenty of moisture. PWATS will be near or\ngreater than 2\" in the valleys with 1-2\" in the mountains, \nwhich is about 1- 2 SD above climo. A surface trough and \nshortwave energy will trigger scattered showers/thunderstorms. \nThe main threat will be brief heavy downpours and gusty winds. \nHowever, shear is on the lower side, so the probability of \nsevere storms is lower. If some storms train over the same area,\nlocalized flash flooding can occur. The pattern does favor the \npossibility for training convection (cloud layer winds nearly \nparallel to low-level wind), but the coverage should be \nisolated. \n\nA similar pattern will hold on for Thursday and Friday while the \nsubtropical jet strengthens to the north and west. Therefore, more \nshowers and thunderstorms are likely along with the threat for \nisolated instances of flash flooding and locally damaging wind \ngusts. Hotter conditions may cause relatively steeper low-level \nlapse rates and the wind field strengthens a bit (especially \nFriday). \n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft will develop for the weekend into next \nweek, bringing more seasonably hot and humid conditions along with \ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms.",
